add(
  'How do you derive finite difference approximations using Taylor series?',
  'Finite difference approximations via Taylor expansion. Forward difference for first derivative: f(x + h) = f(x) + h f\'(x) + h^2/2 f\'\'(x) + h^3/6 f\'\'\'(x) + .... So f\'(x) = (f(x+h) - f(x))/h - h/2 f\'\'(x) + O(h^2). Forward difference: f\'(x) ≈ (f(x+h) - f(x))/h, error O(h) (first-order). Backward: f\'(x) ≈ (f(x) - f(x-h))/h, O(h). Central difference: subtract f(x-h) = f(x) - h f\' + h^2/2 f\'\' - h^3/6 f\'\'\' from f(x+h). (f(x+h) - f(x-h))/(2h) = f\'(x) + h^2/6 f\'\'\'(x) + ... So f\'(x) ≈ (f(x+h) - f(x-h))/(2h), error O(h^2) (second-order; better). Second derivative: add f(x+h) + f(x-h) = 2 f(x) + h^2 f\'\'(x) + h^4/12 f^{(4)}(x) + .... So f\'\'(x) ≈ (f(x+h) - 2 f(x) + f(x-h))/h^2, error O(h^2) (central second difference). Higher-order: use more points (5-point stencil: f\'\' ≈ (-f(x+2h) + 16 f(x+h) - 30 f(x) + 16 f(x-h) - f(x-2h))/(12 h^2), O(h^4)). General: undetermined coefficients. For k-th derivative with n-point stencil, solve linear system for coefficients that cancel lower-order Taylor terms; order = n - k (roughly). Mixed partial: f_{xy} ≈ (f(x+h,y+k) - f(x+h,y-k) - f(x-h,y+k) + f(x-h,y-k))/(4 h k) (central, O(h^2 + k^2)). Non-uniform grid: adjust formulas. Truncation error: leading error term. Round-off: small h amplifies round-off in subtraction; optimal h balances truncation vs round-off (typically h ~ sqrt(eps) for first deriv, eps^{1/3} for second; eps = machine precision ~ 10^{-16}). ✓',
  'ch07p5_fd_taylor',
  'formula_recall',
  ['finite difference', 'Taylor expansion', 'forward backward central', 'truncation error', 'stencil']
)

add(
  'How do you set up the finite difference scheme for the 1D heat equation?',
  'Finite difference for 1D heat equation u_t = alpha u_{xx} on [0, L] with u(0, t) = u(L, t) = 0, u(x, 0) = f(x). Grid: x_j = j Delta x (j = 0, ..., N, Delta x = L/N), t_n = n Delta t. U_j^n ≈ u(x_j, t_n). Discretize: u_t ≈ (U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)/Delta t (forward Euler in time). u_{xx} ≈ (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/(Delta x)^2 (central in space). Scheme (Forward Time Central Space, FTCS): (U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)/Delta t = alpha (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/(Delta x)^2. So U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n + r (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n) where r = alpha Delta t/(Delta x)^2 (Courant number / mesh ratio). Matrix form: U^{n+1} = A U^n where A = I + r T (T = tridiagonal[-1, 2, -1]). With Dirichlet BC: A is (N-1)×(N-1) (interior points). Stability: FTCS is stable iff r <= 1/2 (von Neumann analysis; amplification factor g = 1 - 4 r sin^2(k Delta x/2), |g| <= 1 iff r <= 1/2). So Delta t <= Delta x^2/(2 alpha) (severe restriction: halving Delta x requires quartering Delta t). Backward Euler (BTCS): (U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)/Delta t = alpha (U_{j+1}^{n+1} - 2 U_j^{n+1} + U_{j-1}^{n+1})/(Delta x)^2. Implicit: U^{n+1} - r T U^{n+1} = U^n, i.e., (I - r T) U^{n+1} = U^n. Solve tridiagonal system each step. Unconditionally stable (any Delta t). Crank-Nicolson: average of FTCS and BTCS: (U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)/Delta t = (alpha/2) [(U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/(Delta x)^2 + (U_{j+1}^{n+1} - 2 U_j^{n+1} + U_{j-1}^{n+1})/(Delta x)^2]. Implicit, unconditionally stable, second-order in time AND space (O(Delta t^2 + Delta x^2)). ✓',
  'ch07p5_fd_heat_equation',
  'problem_solving',
  ['finite difference', 'heat equation', 'FTCS BTCS', 'Crank-Nicolson', 'Courant number r']
)

add(
  'How do you set up the finite difference scheme for the 1D wave equation?',
  'Finite difference for 1D wave equation u_{tt} = c^2 u_{xx} on [0, L], u(0,t) = u(L,t) = 0, u(x, 0) = f(x), u_t(x, 0) = g(x). Grid: x_j = j Delta x, t_n = n Delta t. Central differences in both: u_{tt} ≈ (U_j^{n+1} - 2 U_j^n + U_j^{n-1})/(Delta t)^2. u_{xx} ≈ (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/(Delta x)^2. Scheme: (U_j^{n+1} - 2 U_j^n + U_j^{n-1})/(Delta t)^2 = c^2 (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/(Delta x)^2. Let rho = c Delta t/Delta x (Courant number). U_j^{n+1} = 2 U_j^n - U_j^{n-1} + rho^2 (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n). Three-level scheme (needs U^0 and U^1 to start). Initial step U^1 from u(x, 0) = f and u_t(x, 0) = g: U_j^1 = f(x_j) + Delta t g(x_j) + (Delta t^2/2) c^2 f\'\'(x_j) (using Taylor + PDE). Or U_j^1 = f(x_j) + Delta t g(x_j) + (rho^2/2) (f(x_{j+1}) - 2 f(x_j) + f(x_{j-1})) (discretized). Stability (CFL): rho <= 1 (Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy condition). c Delta t <= Delta x (information cannot travel more than one grid point per time step). If rho = 1: exact for traveling waves (U_j^{n+1} = U_{j+1}^n + U_{j-1}^n - U_j^{n-1}, captures F(x+ct) + G(x-ct) exactly). If rho > 1: unstable (amplification grows). Conservation: discrete energy E^n = (1/2) sum [(U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)^2/Delta t^2 + c^2 (U_{j+1}^n - U_j^n)^2/Delta x^2] Delta x conserved (for rho <= 1). Higher dimensions: u_{tt} = c^2 (u_{xx} + u_{yy}) -> CFL: c Delta t sqrt(1/Delta x^2 + 1/Delta y^2) <= 1. Leapfrog scheme (3-level central). Lax-Wendroff (second-order, adds artificial diffusion term for stability). ✓',
  'ch07p5_fd_wave_equation',
  'problem_solving',
  ['finite difference', 'wave equation', 'three-level scheme', 'CFL condition', 'Courant number rho']
)

add(
  'How do you set up the finite difference scheme for Laplace and Poisson equations?',
  'Finite difference for Laplace Delta u = 0 (or Poisson Delta u = f) on rectangular domain. 2D grid x_i = i Delta x, y_j = j Delta y. 5-point stencil for Laplacian: Delta u ≈ (u_{i+1,j} - 2 u_{i,j} + u_{i-1,j})/Delta x^2 + (u_{i,j+1} - 2 u_{i,j} + u_{i,j-1})/Delta y^2. For Delta x = Delta y = h: Delta u ≈ (u_{i+1,j} + u_{i-1,j} + u_{i,j+1} + u_{i,j-1} - 4 u_{i,j})/h^2 (5-point stencil). Error O(h^2). Poisson: (u_{i+1,j} + u_{i-1,j} + u_{i,j+1} + u_{i,j-1} - 4 u_{i,j})/h^2 = f_{i,j}. Jacobi iteration: u_{i,j}^{(k+1)} = (1/4)(u_{i+1,j}^{(k)} + u_{i-1,j}^{(k)} + u_{i,j+1}^{(k)} + u_{i,j-1}^{(k)} - h^2 f_{i,j}). Gauss-Seidel: use updated values immediately: u_{i,j}^{(k+1)} = (1/4)(u_{i+1,j}^{(k)} + u_{i-1,j}^{(k+1)} + u_{i,j+1}^{(k)} + u_{i,j-1}^{(k+1)} - h^2 f_{i,j}) (faster than Jacobi). SOR (Successive Over-Relaxation): u_{i,j}^{(k+1)} = (1 - omega) u_{i,j}^{(k)} + omega · (Gauss-Seidel update), with omega in (1, 2) (over-relaxation; optimal omega = 2/(1 + sin(pi h)) for square domain, gives O(N) iterations instead of O(N^2) for Jacobi). Convergence: Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel converge for diagonally dominant matrices (Laplacian is). Rate: Jacobi O(N^2) iterations, Gauss-Seidel 2x faster, SOR O(N) with optimal omega. Multigrid: O(N) total work (optimal). Direct solvers: banded Gaussian elimination O(N^3) (prohibitive for large N); sparse LU (O(N^{1.5}) for 2D, O(N^2) for 3D); FFT-based (O(N log N) for separable problems). 9-point stencil: higher accuracy O(h^4) for Laplace (combines 5-point with rotated 5-point). Non-uniform grid: finite volume or box method. Curved boundaries: embedded boundary, immersed boundary, cut-cell methods. ✓',
  'ch07p5_fd_laplace_poisson',
  'problem_solving',
  ['finite difference', 'Laplace Poisson', '5-point stencil', 'Jacobi Gauss-Seidel SOR', 'multigrid']
)

add(
  'What is the Lax-Wendroff scheme for hyperbolic equations?',
  'Lax-Wendroff scheme for advection u_t + a u_x = 0 (a constant). Second-order accurate in space and time. Derivation via Taylor: u(x, t + Delta t) = u + Delta t u_t + (Delta t^2/2) u_{tt} + O(Delta t^3). Use PDE: u_t = -a u_x, u_{tt} = a^2 u_{xx} (from u_t = -a u_x => u_{tt} = -a u_{xt} = -a (u_t)_x = -a (-a u_x)_x = a^2 u_{xx}). So u(x, t + Delta t) = u - a Delta t u_x + (a^2 Delta t^2/2) u_{xx} + .... Discretize: U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - (a Delta t/(2 Delta x)) (U_{j+1}^n - U_{j-1}^n) + (a^2 Delta t^2/(2 Delta x^2)) (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n). Let mu = a Delta t/Delta x (Courant number). U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - (mu/2)(U_{j+1}^n - U_{j-1}^n) + (mu^2/2)(U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n). Rewrite: U_j^{n+1} = (mu^2/2 - mu/2) U_{j+1}^n + (1 - mu^2) U_j^n + (mu^2/2 + mu/2) U_{j-1}^n. Stability (von Neumann): |mu| <= 1 (CFL condition; same as upwind). Truncation: O(Delta t^2 + Delta x^2) (second-order). Dissipation: numerical diffusion (a^2 Delta t^2/2 u_{xx} term) damps high frequencies. Dispersion: phase error O(Delta x^2) (Lax-Wendroff is dissipative but dispersive; oscillations behind sharp fronts — Godunov\'s theorem: linear schemes > first-order cannot be monotone for shocks). Two-step Lax-Wendroff (predictor-corrector): U_{j+1/2}^{n+1/2} = (1/2)(U_{j+1}^n + U_j^n) - (Delta t/(2 Delta x)) (F(U_{j+1}^n) - F(U_j^n)) (half-step). U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - (Delta t/Delta x)(F(U_{j+1/2}^{n+1/2}) - F(U_{j-1/2}^{n+1/2})). For nonlinear conservation u_t + F(u)_x = 0. MacCormack scheme: predictor U*_j = U_j^n - (Delta t/Delta x)(F(U_{j+1}^n) - F(U_j^n)); corrector U_j^{n+1} = (1/2)(U_j^n + U*_j) - (Delta t/(2 Delta x))(F(U*_j) - F(U*_{j-1})). ✓',
  'ch07p5_lax_wendroff',
  'problem_solving',
  ['Lax-Wendroff', 'advection', 'second-order', 'Taylor derivation', 'predictor-corrector']
)

add(
  'What are upwind schemes and why are they used for advection equations?',
  'Upwind schemes for advection u_t + a u_x = 0 (a constant velocity). The flow of information is in the direction of a: if a > 0, information flows from left to right (left is "upwind"); if a < 0, right to left. Upwind discretization uses differencing biased toward the upwind direction. If a > 0: u_x ≈ (U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n)/Delta x (backward difference). Scheme: U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - (a Delta t/Delta x)(U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n) = U_j^n - mu (U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n) where mu = a Delta t/Delta x. If a < 0: u_x ≈ (U_{j+1}^n - U_j^n)/Delta x (forward difference). Scheme: U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - mu (U_{j+1}^n - U_j^n). General: U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - mu (U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n) if a > 0, else U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - mu (U_{j+1}^n - U_j^n). Stability (von Neumann): amplification g = 1 - mu (1 - e^{-i k Delta x}) for a > 0. |g|^2 = (1 - mu + mu cos(k Delta x))^2 + (mu sin(k Delta x))^2 = 1 - 2 mu (1 - mu) (1 - cos(k Delta x)). |g|^2 <= 1 iff mu (1 - mu) >= 0, i.e., 0 <= mu <= 1 (CFL). So |mu| = |a| Delta t/Delta x <= 1. First-order accurate (O(Delta t + Delta x)). Numerical diffusion: upwind has artificial viscosity nu_art = (|a| Delta x/2)(1 - |mu|) (from modified equation analysis); damps high frequencies (good for stability, bad for sharp fronts). Properties: (1) Monotone (no spurious oscillations; TVD = total variation diminishing). (2) Captures shocks without oscillation (but smears them over ~Delta x). (3) Godunov\'s theorem: linear monotone schemes are at most first-order (can\'t have both high-order and monotone). Nonlinear upwind (MUSCL, WENO, ENO): higher-order where smooth, first-order near shocks (TVD, ENO, WENO reconstructions). Riemann solvers (Godunov, Roe, HLL): exact/approximate Riemann at each cell interface. Flux-limiter (TVD): phi(r) blends first-order (upwind) and second-order (Lax-Wendroff) based on smoothness indicator r. ✓',
  'ch07p5_upwind_schemes',
  'problem_solving',
  ['upwind scheme', 'advection', 'CFL', 'numerical diffusion', 'TVD monotone']
)

add(
  'How do you handle boundary conditions in finite difference methods?',
  'Boundary conditions in FD methods. Dirichlet (u specified on boundary): simply set U_j^n = g(x_j, t_n) at boundary grid points (rows of the system removed; RHS adjusted). For 1D heat eq on [0, L] with u(0, t) = g_0, u(L, t) = g_L: U_0^n = g_0(t_n), U_N^n = g_L(t_n); interior points j = 1, ..., N-1 solved by the scheme. Neumann (partial u/partial n specified): use ghost points or one-sided differences. Ghost point method: introduce U_{-1} (fictitious point outside). partial u/partial x(0, t) = h => (U_1 - U_{-1})/(2 Delta x) = h (central at boundary) => U_{-1} = U_1 - 2 Delta x · h. Substitute into the scheme at j = 0. Or one-sided: (U_1 - U_0)/Delta x = h (forward, first-order); (3 U_0 - 4 U_1 + U_2)/(2 Delta x) = h (second-order one-sided). Robin (a u + b partial u/partial n = g): combine the above; e.g., a U_0 + b (U_1 - U_{-1})/(2 Delta x) = g, solve for U_{-1}. Time-dependent BC: update at each time step. Curved boundary (non-grid-aligned): immersed boundary method (force term at boundary), cut-cell (reshape cells near boundary), embedded boundary (modify stencil near boundary). Higher-order BC: use one-sided stencils of matching order. Compatibility at corners: for smooth solution, BC and IC must agree at corner (e.g., f(0) = g_0(0)); else corner singularity. Periodic BC: U_0 = U_N (wrap around); use circular tridiagonal solver. Outflow / radiation BC: absorbing (partial u/partial t + c partial u/partial x = 0 at outflow), perfectly matched layers (PML), sponge layers. Nonlocal BC (fractional Laplacian): special techniques (integral over boundary, fast multipole). Adaptive mesh refinement (AMR): grid refinement near boundaries/features (Berger-Oliger, block-structured). ✓',
  'ch07p5_fd_boundary_conditions',
  'formula_recall',
  ['boundary conditions FD', 'Dirichlet Neumann Robin', 'ghost point', 'one-sided difference', 'immersed boundary']
)

