add(
  'What is the Lax-Richtmyer equivalence theorem for finite difference schemes?',
  'Lax-Richtmyer equivalence theorem (1956). For a well-posed linear initial value problem (IVP) and a consistent finite difference scheme, the scheme is CONVERGENT if and only if it is STABLE. Statement: let the PDE be u_t = L u with u(0) = f (well-posed: ||u(t)|| <= C e^{a t} ||f||), discretized as U^{n+1} = C(Delta t, Delta x) U^n (one-step scheme; or multi-step). Consistency: C(Delta t, Delta x) -> e^{Delta t L} as Delta t, Delta x -> 0 (local truncation error tau = (C(Delta t) u(t_n) - u(t_{n+1}))/Delta t -> 0). Stability: ||C(Delta t)^n|| <= K e^{a T} for all n Delta t <= T (uniform bound on powers; equivalently, amplification operator bounded). Then: convergence ||U^n - u(t_n)|| -> 0 as Delta t -> 0 iff stability holds. Convergence rate = order of consistency (e.g., O(Delta t^p + Delta x^q) consistent + stable => O(Delta t^p + Delta x^q) convergent). Significance: reduces convergence (hard to prove directly) to stability + consistency (easier). Foundation of numerical PDE theory. Caveats: (1) Linear only (nonlinear needs separate analysis; often linearize + stability of linearized). (2) IVP (initial value); BVP (boundary value) has separate theory. (3) Requires well-posedness of PDE. Generalizations: Kreiss matrix theorem (stability equivalences), Gustafsson-Sundqvist-Kreiss (boundary stability), Strang theorem (nonlinear). For nonlinear hyperbolic (conservation laws): convergence theory harder (TVD, ENO, WENO; convergence to entropy solution; Bressan, Bressan-LeFloch). For elliptic BVP: equivalence via coercivity (Lax-Milgram) + Galerkin convergence (Cea\'s lemma). ✓',
  'ch07p5_lax_richtmyer',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Lax-Richtmyer', 'equivalence theorem', 'consistency stability convergence', 'linear IVP', 'well-posed']
)

add(
  'What is consistency of a finite difference scheme and how do you check it?',
  'Consistency of a finite difference scheme. Definition: a scheme U^{n+1} = Q(Delta t, Delta x) U^n approximating u_t = L u is consistent if the local truncation error tau = (Q(Delta t) u(t_n) - u(t_{n+1}))/Delta t -> 0 as Delta t, Delta x -> 0 (when u is a smooth exact solution of the PDE). Equivalently: substitute the exact PDE solution u into the scheme; the residual (after dividing by Delta t) goes to zero. Order of accuracy: scheme is order (p, q) if tau = O(Delta t^p + Delta x^q). Example: FTCS for heat eq u_t = alpha u_{xx}. Scheme: (U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)/Delta t = alpha (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/Delta x^2. Substitute exact u: tau = (u_j^{n+1} - u_j^n)/Delta t - alpha (u_{j+1}^n - 2 u_j^n + u_{j-1}^n)/Delta x^2. Taylor: u_j^{n+1} = u + Delta t u_t + (Delta t^2/2) u_{tt} + ..., u_{j+1}^n = u + Delta x u_x + (Delta x^2/2) u_{xx} + (Delta x^3/6) u_{xxx} + (Delta x^4/24) u_{xxxx} + .... So (u_j^{n+1} - u_j^n)/Delta t = u_t + (Delta t/2) u_{tt} + O(Delta t^2). And (u_{j+1} - 2 u_j + u_{j-1})/Delta x^2 = u_{xx} + (Delta x^2/12) u_{xxxx} + O(Delta x^4). So tau = u_t + (Delta t/2) u_{tt} - alpha u_{xx} - alpha (Delta x^2/12) u_{xxxx} + ... = (Delta t/2) u_{tt} - (alpha Delta x^2/12) u_{xxxx} + ... (using PDE u_t = alpha u_{xx}). So tau = O(Delta t + Delta x^2): FTCS is first-order in time, second-order in space. Crank-Nicolson: O(Delta t^2 + Delta x^2) (second-order both). Check: substitute, Taylor expand, collect leading error terms. Lax-Wendroff: O(Delta t^2 + Delta x^2) (designed by including Delta t^2 correction). Consistency is necessary but not sufficient for convergence (need stability; Lax-Richtmyer). ✓',
  'ch07p5_consistency_check',
  'formula_recall',
  ['consistency', 'local truncation error', 'Taylor expansion', 'order of accuracy', 'substitute exact solution']
)

add(
  'What is stability of a finite difference scheme and how is it analyzed?',
  'Stability of a finite difference scheme. Definition: a one-step scheme U^{n+1} = Q(Delta t) U^n is stable if there exist constants K > 0 and a such that ||Q(Delta t)^n|| <= K e^{a T} for all n with n Delta t <= T (uniform bound on powers of Q, independent of Delta t). Equivalently: small perturbations in initial data do not grow unboundedly (in finite time T). Types: (1) Zero-stability (fixed Delta t, n -> inf with T fixed). (2) Absolute stability (specific Delta t, all n). (3) Lax-Richtmyer stability (uniform over Delta t -> 0). Methods to check stability: (a) Von Neumann (Fourier) analysis: assume U_j^n = g^n e^{i k j Delta x}, derive amplification factor g(k). Scheme stable iff |g(k)| <= 1 + C Delta t for all k (von Neumann condition). Works for constant-coefficient schemes on infinite/uniform grid. (b) Matrix method: write U^{n+1} = A U^n, check ||A^n|| <= K (eigenvalue: |lambda_max(A)| <= 1 + C Delta t; for normal A, sufficient; for non-normal, need pseudospectrum). (c) Energy method: define discrete energy E^n, show E^{n+1} <= E^n (or <= (1 + C Delta t) E^n). Works for variable coefficients, nonlinear. (d) CFL condition (necessary, not sufficient): information propagation speed in scheme >= PDE speed (Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy). Examples: FTCS heat eq: |g| = |1 - 4 r sin^2(k Delta x/2)| <= 1 iff r <= 1/2 (stable). Upwind advection: |g|^2 = 1 - 2 mu (1 - mu)(1 - cos(k Delta x)) <= 1 iff 0 <= mu <= 1 (CFL). Crank-Nicolson: |g| = |(1 - 2 r sin^2/2)/(1 + 2 r sin^2/2)| <= 1 for all r (unconditionally stable). Backward Euler heat: |g| = |1/(1 + 4 r sin^2/2)| <= 1 (unconditionally stable). Strict vs practical stability: |g| <= 1 (strict); |g| <= 1 + C Delta t (practical, sufficient for Lax-Richtmyer). ✓',
  'ch07p5_stability_analysis',
  'formula_recall',
  ['stability', 'amplification factor', 'von Neumann', 'energy method', 'CFL necessary']
)

add(
  'What is the von Neumann stability analysis and how do you apply it?',
  'Von Neumann (Fourier) stability analysis. Setup: linear constant-coefficient finite difference scheme on uniform grid, infinite or periodic domain. Ansatz: U_j^n = g^n e^{i k j Delta x} (Fourier mode with wavenumber k, amplification factor g per time step). Substitute into scheme; solve for g as function of k, Delta t, Delta x. Stability: |g(k)| <= 1 + C Delta t for all k in the resolved range (|k| <= pi/Delta x by Nyquist) — von Neumann condition. (For Delta t -> 0, this becomes |g| <= 1.) Example 1 (FTCS heat): (U_j^{n+1} - U_j^n)/Delta t = alpha (U_{j+1}^n - 2 U_j^n + U_{j-1}^n)/Delta x^2. Substitute U_j^n = g^n e^{i k j Delta x}: g - 1 = r (e^{i k Delta x} - 2 + e^{-i k Delta x}) = r (2 cos(k Delta x) - 2) = -4 r sin^2(k Delta x/2). So g = 1 - 4 r sin^2(k Delta x/2). |g| <= 1 iff 0 <= 4 r sin^2 <= 2 (since 1 - x >= -1 requires x <= 2, and 1 - x <= 1 requires x >= 0). Max of sin^2 is 1, so 4 r <= 2, r <= 1/2. Stable iff r = alpha Delta t/Delta x^2 <= 1/2. Example 2 (upwind advection u_t + a u_x = 0, a > 0): U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - mu (U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n), mu = a Delta t/Delta x. g = 1 - mu (1 - e^{-i k Delta x}) = 1 - mu + mu (cos(k Delta x) - i sin(k Delta x)). |g|^2 = (1 - mu + mu cos)^2 + mu^2 sin^2 = (1 - mu)^2 + 2 mu (1 - mu) cos + mu^2 = 1 - 2 mu + 2 mu^2 + 2 mu (1 - mu) cos. = 1 - 2 mu (1 - mu) (1 - cos). Max at cos = -1: |g|^2 = 1 - 4 mu (1 - mu) <= 1 iff mu (1 - mu) >= 0, i.e., 0 <= mu <= 1. So CFL |mu| <= 1. Example 3 (Crank-Nicolson heat): g = (1 - 2 r sin^2/2)/(1 + 2 r sin^2/2), |g| <= 1 always (numerator smaller than denominator in abs). Unconditionally stable. Limitations: only for linear constant-coefficient on uniform grid; for variable coefficients or nonlinear, use energy method or freeze coefficients (heuristic). ✓',
  'ch07p5_von_neumann_analysis',
  'problem_solving',
  ['von Neumann', 'Fourier stability', 'amplification factor', 'FTCS upwind Crank-Nicolson', 'CFL']
)

add(
  'What is the CFL (Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy) condition?',
  'CFL condition (Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy, 1928). Necessary condition for convergence of a finite difference scheme for a PDE: the domain of dependence of the numerical scheme (at a grid point) must CONTAIN the domain of dependence of the PDE. PDE domain of dependence: for hyperbolic PDE (wave eq, advection), the set of initial data points that influence u(x_0, t_0). For advection u_t + a u_x = 0: a single characteristic x - a t = const, so domain of dependence of (x_0, t_0) is the single point x_0 - a t_0 (on t = 0). For wave u_{tt} = c^2 u_{xx}: interval [x_0 - c t_0, x_0 + c t_0] (characteristic cone). Numerical domain of dependence: set of grid points at t = 0 that influence U(x_0, t_0) via the scheme. For upwind advection: U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - mu (U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n); each step looks at j and j-1. After n steps (t_0 = n Delta t), the influence comes from grid points x_{j-n} to x_j, i.e., x_0 - n Delta x = x_0 - (t_0/Delta t) Delta x to x_0. PDE domain: x_0 - a t_0. CFL: x_0 - a t_0 in [x_0 - n Delta x, x_0], i.e., a t_0 <= n Delta x = (t_0/Delta t) Delta x, i.e., a Delta t <= Delta x, i.e., mu = a Delta t/Delta x <= 1. For a < 0: similar, |a| Delta t <= Delta x. Wave eq 1D: c Delta t/Delta x <= 1 (rho <= 1). 2D wave: c Delta t sqrt(1/Delta x^2 + 1/Delta y^2) <= 1. Heat eq (parabolic): infinite PDE propagation speed, so CFL is automatically satisfied for any Delta t (the necessary condition is trivially true; actual stability requires stronger condition like r <= 1/2 for FTCS). CFL is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT (e.g., FTCS heat satisfies CFL trivially but unstable for r > 1/2). For hyperbolic: CFL usually also sufficient for linear schemes (with von Neumann). Physical interpretation: numerical scheme must propagate information at least as fast as PDE; if scheme too slow (Delta t too large for given Delta x), it can\'t "see" the relevant initial data, and can\'t converge. Generalization: CFL for systems (max eigenvalue), for nonlinear (freeze coefficients), for FEM (element-wise CFL). ✓',
  'ch07p5_cfl_condition',
  'formula_recall',
  ['CFL', 'Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy', 'domain of dependence', 'necessary condition', 'hyperbolic PDE']
)

add(
  'What is the modified equation analysis for finite difference schemes?',
  'Modified equation analysis (Warming-Hyett 1974). Idea: the finite difference scheme is an EXACT solution to a MODIFIED PDE (the PDE that the scheme solves exactly), which differs from the original PDE by truncation terms. Analysis: derive the modified PDE by substituting Taylor expansion (assuming smooth solution) and eliminating time derivatives using the PDE itself. Example: upwind scheme for u_t + a u_x = 0, U_j^{n+1} = U_j^n - mu (U_j^n - U_{j-1}^n). Substitute smooth u: u + Delta t u_t + (Delta t^2/2) u_{tt} + ... = u - mu (u - (u - Delta x u_x + Delta x^2/2 u_{xx} - ...)) = u - mu (Delta x u_x - Delta x^2/2 u_{xx} + Delta x^3/6 u_{xxx} - ...). Use mu = a Delta t/Delta x. Divide by Delta t: u_t + (Delta t/2) u_{tt} + ... = -a u_x + (a Delta x/2) u_{xx} - (a Delta x^2/6) u_{xxx} + .... Eliminate u_{tt} = -a u_{xt} = a^2 u_{xx} (using PDE u_t = -a u_x; at lowest order). So u_t + (Delta t/2) a^2 u_{xx} + ... = -a u_x + (a Delta x/2) u_{xx} + .... Rearrange: u_t + a u_x = (a Delta x/2 - a^2 Delta t/2) u_{xx} + ... = (a Delta x/2)(1 - mu) u_{xx} + higher-order. Modified equation: u_t + a u_x = (a Delta x/2)(1 - mu) u_{xx} + O(Delta x^2, Delta t^2). The upwind scheme is a first-order accurate solver of u_t + a u_x = (a Delta x/2)(1 - mu) u_{xx} (advection-diffusion with artificial viscosity nu = (a Delta x/2)(1 - mu) >= 0 for 0 <= mu <= 1). This explains: (1) Upwind is stable (artificial diffusion damps high frequencies). (2) Upwind smears shocks (diffusive). (3) mu = 1 gives zero diffusion (exact for pure advection). Lax-Wendroff modified eq: u_t + a u_x = -(a Delta x^2/6)(1 - mu^2) u_{xxx} + ... (dispersive, third-derivative; causes phase errors, oscillations behind shocks). Lax-Friedrichs: stronger artificial diffusion. Applications: (a) Understand numerical behavior (diffusion vs dispersion). (b) Design schemes (add counter-diffusion for anti-diffusive corrections; flux-limiter). (c) Stability: artificial viscosity >= 0 implies stability. (d) Convergence: scheme converges to modified PDE; modified PDE -> original as Delta x -> 0 (consistency). ✓',
  'ch07p5_modified_equation',
  'formula_recall',
  ['modified equation', 'Warming-Hyett', 'artificial viscosity', 'numerical diffusion', 'dispersion error']
)

