add(
  'What is the Fourier spectral method for PDEs with periodic boundary conditions?',
  'Fourier spectral method. For periodic BC on [0, 2 pi] (or torus T^n). Approximate u(x) ≈ u_N(x) = sum_{k = -N/2}^{N/2} hat u_k e^{i k x} (truncated Fourier series). Derivatives: du/dx ≈ sum i k hat u_k e^{i k x} (multiply by i k in Fourier; exact for spectral accuracy). PDE u_t = L u: in Fourier, hat u_k\'(t) = L_k hat u_k(t) (decoupled ODEs; for linear L). Solve each ODE. Nonlinear: pseudo-spectral (compute nonlinear term in physical space). Pseudospectral (collocation): (1) Represent u on grid x_j = 2 pi j/N (j = 0, ..., N-1). (2) Compute u(x_j) (function values). (3) FFT to get hat u_k. (4) Multiply by (ik)^m for m-th derivative. (5) Inverse FFT to get derivative values at grid. (6) Compute nonlinear term at grid points (no aliasing if dealiased; 2/3 rule: zero out top 1/3 modes). Time-stepping: RK4, IMEX (implicit for stiff linear, explicit for non-stiff nonlinear). Stability: explicit time step Delta t <= C/N^2 (CFL for diffusion; spectral makes Delta x ~ 1/N, so Delta t ~ 1/N^2); for advection: Delta t <= C/N. Aliasing: nonlinear product in physical space creates modes beyond N (alias back into [-N/2, N/2]); dealias by zero-padding (2/3 rule: keep modes |k| <= N/3, zero rest). Convergence: exponential (||u - u_N|| <= C e^{-sigma N} ||u||) for analytic u; algebraic (||u - u_N|| ~ N^{-s}) for u in H^s. Spectral accuracy: error decreases faster than any polynomial for C^inf data. Advantages: extreme accuracy for smooth solutions, low numerical diffusion/dispersion. Disadvantages: requires smooth solution and simple geometry (periodic or tensor-product). Applications: turbulence DNS (incompressible Navier-Stokes), weather/climate (spectral transform method), plasma physics, quantum mechanics (Bose-Einstein condensate via Gross-Pitaevskii), nonlinear waves. Non-periodic: Chebyshev or Legendre spectral (next item). ✓',
  'ch07p5_fourier_spectral',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Fourier spectral', 'pseudospectral', 'FFT', 'periodic BC', 'exponential convergence']
)

add(
  'What is the Chebyshev spectral method for non-periodic boundary value problems?',
  'Chebyshev spectral method for non-periodic BVPs on [-1, 1]. Basis: Chebyshev polynomials T_k(x) = cos(k arccos x), k = 0, 1, .... Orthogonal w.r.t. weight 1/sqrt(1 - x^2): integral_{-1}^1 T_k(x) T_m(x)/sqrt(1-x^2) dx = (pi/2) delta_{km} (k, m >= 1); pi for k = m = 0. Approximate u(x) ≈ u_N(x) = sum_{k=0}^N a_k T_k(x). Collocation points: x_j = cos(j pi/N), j = 0, ..., N (Chebyshev-Gauss-Lobatto; cluster near endpoints). Derivative matrix D_{jm} = dT_k/dx(x_j) (compute from recurrence). Explicit: D^{(1)}_{jm} = ... has closed form (Trefethen). Higher derivatives: D^{(m)} = (D^{(1)})^m (matrix power). Solve BVP: discretize, apply BCs (rows for boundary points), solve linear system. Convergence: exponential for analytic u (||u - u_N|| <= C rho^{-N} ||u||, rho > 1 related to largest Bernstein ellipse where u analytic). Aliasing: Chebyshev grid aliasing (deal with 2/3 rule analog). BC: Dirichlet (set rows for boundary points to BC values); Neumann (use derivative matrix or tau method). Tau method: enforce BCs via projecting residual onto lower modes. Collocation: enforce BCs by setting u_N(x_0) = g_0, u_N(x_N) = g_N (replacing two equations). Chebyshev vs Legendre: both work; Chebyshev has FFT (fast), Legendre has simpler Gauss quadrature. Chebyshev-Gauss quadrature: integral_{-1}^1 f(x)/sqrt(1-x^2) dx ≈ (pi/N) sum_{j=1}^N f(x_j) (Chebyshev nodes of first kind x_j = cos((2j-1)pi/(2N))). Applications: boundary layers (high resolution near walls), eigenvalue problems (Orr-Sommerfeld for stability), optimal control, spectral element (tensor product on simple elements). Chebyshev on [a, b]: affine transform x -> (2x - a - b)/(b - a). Multi-dimensional: tensor product T_k(x) T_m(y) on rectangle; on triangle use Dubiner basis (collapsing coordinates). Preconditioned iterative solvers: finite-difference or finite-element preconditioner for spectral (Orszag). Time-stepping: explicit Delta t <= C/N^2 (CFL; spectral smallest grid spacing ~ 1/N^2 near endpoints). Implicit for stiff. ✓',
  'ch07p5_chebyshev_spectral',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Chebyshev spectral', 'non-periodic BVP', 'Chebyshev polynomials', 'collocation points', 'exponential convergence']
)

add(
  'What is the pseudospectral method and how do you handle nonlinear terms?',
  'Pseudospectral (collocation) method for nonlinear PDEs. Setup: PDE u_t = L u + N(u) where L linear, N nonlinear. Spectral representation u_N = sum hat u_k e^{ikx} (Fourier; or Chebyshev). Linear part: in spectral space, hat u_k\' = L_k hat u_k (diagonal; easy). Nonlinear part N(u): pseudospectral evaluation. (1) Compute u_N(x_j) at grid points (inverse FFT from hat u_k). (2) Compute N(u_N(x_j)) pointwise (in physical space; e.g., u^2 = u_N(x_j) * u_N(x_j)). (3) FFT N(u_N) back to spectral: hat N_k. (4) Update hat u_k\' = L_k hat u_k + hat N_k. Time step: explicit (RK4) for nonlinear, implicit for linear (IMEX): hat u_k^{n+1} = (hat u_k^n + Delta t hat N_k^n)/(1 - Delta t L_k) (linear implicit). Why pseudospectral (vs pure Galerkin)? Nonlinear products in spectral space are convolutions (O(N^2) per step); in physical space pointwise (O(N) + FFT O(N log N), much faster). Aliasing: nonlinear product creates modes beyond N (e.g., e^{ikx}·e^{imx} = e^{i(k+m)x}, but k+m may exceed N/2, aliasing back). Dealiasing: (a) 3/2 rule (zero-pad to 3N/2 modes before product, then truncate). (b) 2/3 rule (zero out modes |k| > N/3 after each step; removes aliased modes). (c) Phase shifting (less common). Without dealiasing: nonlinear instability may develop. Orszag two-thirds rule: keep modes |k| <= N/3, zero N/3 < |k| <= N/2; loses some resolution but stable. Applications: incompressible Navier-Stokes (pseudo-spectral with projection for incompressibility; vorticity-streamfunction or primitive variables + pressure projection), turbulence (DNS, 1024^3 modes), MHD, Boussinesq convection, nonlinear Schrodinger (split-step Fourier: linear part exactly in Fourier, nonlinear part in physical). Convergence: spectral (exponential) for smooth u. Cost: O(N log N) per step (FFT). Stability: explicit Delta t <= C/N (advection) or C/N^2 (diffusion); semi-implicit relaxes this. Resolution: N ~ 10^2-10^3 per dimension typical; petascale DNS N ~ 10^4. ✓',
  'ch07p5_pseudospectral_nonlinear',
  'problem_solving',
  ['pseudospectral', 'nonlinear', 'collocation', 'aliasing dealiasing', 'Orszag rule']
)

add(
  'What are spectral methods for the incompressible Navier-Stokes equations?',
  'Spectral methods for incompressible Navier-Stokes. Equations: u_t + (u · grad) u = -grad p + nu Delta u + f (momentum), div u = 0 (incompressibility), in periodic box [0, 2 pi]^3 (homogeneous turbulence) or with no-slip walls (channel flow). Projection method (Chorin 1968): (1) Intermediate velocity u* = u^n + Delta t [-((u·grad)u)^n + nu Delta u^n + f^n] (no pressure). (2) Projection: u^{n+1} = u* - Delta t grad p, with div u^{n+1} = 0. Take divergence: 0 = div u* - Delta t Delta p => Delta p = div u*/Delta t (Poisson for pressure; solve in Fourier: hat p_k = i k · hat u*_k/(Delta t |k|^2)). (3) u^{n+1} = u* - Delta t grad p (project onto divergence-free). In Fourier (periodic): each mode k != 0 has projection P_k = I - k k^T/|k|^2 (Leray projection). hat u_k = P_k hat u_k (removes compressible part). Nonlinear term: pseudospectral. (u·grad)u = div(u ⊗ u) (conservative form; better conservation). Compute u ⊗ u in physical, FFT, project. Convection form (u·grad)u: pseudospectral. Skew-symmetric form (combines both; less aliasing). Vorticity-streamfunction (2D): omega = curl u, psi streamfunction (u = curl psi). omega_t + (u·grad) omega = nu Delta omega. Poisson: Delta psi = -omega. Solve in Fourier: hat omega_k\' = -FFT[(u·grad)omega] - nu |k|^2 hat omega_k. psi from omega via hat psi_k = -hat omega_k/|k|^2. u = curl psi in Fourier: hat u_k = i k_perp hat psi_k (2D). Boundary: no-slip walls require Chebyshev in wall-normal direction, Fourier in streamwise/spanwise (Kim-Moin-Moser 1987 for channel). Time-stepping: Crank-Nicolson for viscous (implicit, stiff), Adams-Bashforth for convective (explicit). Dealiasing: 2/3 rule. Resolution: Re_tau = 180 channel needs 128^3; Re_tau = 1000 needs 1024^3; Re_tau = 5000 needs 8192^3 (petascale). Stability: CFL Delta t <= C min(h/|u|, h^2/nu) (viscous and convective). Pressure: exact projection or iterative (Uzawa, SIMPLE). ✓',
  'ch07p5_spectral_navier_stokes',
  'problem_solving',
  ['spectral Navier-Stokes', 'incompressible', 'projection method', 'Leray projection', 'vorticity streamfunction']
)

add(
  'How do you choose between finite difference, finite element, and spectral methods?',
  'Choosing between FD, FEM, spectral. Factors: (1) Geometry: simple (rectangular, periodic) -> FD or spectral (Fourier). Complex (curved, unstructured) -> FEM (handles arbitrary triangulations) or spectral element. (2) Smoothness: smooth/analytic solution -> spectral (exponential convergence). Rough/discontinuous (shocks, corners) -> low-order FEM/FD (spectral Gibbs oscillations near discontinuities). (3) Accuracy needed: low (1-5%) -> FD. Medium -> FEM. High (spectral accuracy for turbulence, quantum) -> spectral. (4) Cost: FD cheapest per DOF (sparse, local). FEM sparse but more setup (mesh, assembly). Spectral dense O(N^2)-O(N^3) but N small for given accuracy; FFT helps (O(N log N)). (5) Boundary conditions: complex BC, mixed, Robin -> FEM (natural handling via weak form). Simple -> FD. Spectral needs special treatment (tau, penalty). (6) Conservation: FEM good for conservation (Galerkin). DG excellent (local conservation). FD with finite-volume flux form. Spectral in Fourier: spectral conservation. (7) Adaptivity: hp-FEM best (combined h and p refinement). FD h-refinement with AMR (Berger-Oliger). Spectral: limited adaptivity (domain decomposition). (8) Stability: FD upwind/TVD for shocks. FEM stabilized (SUPG, GLS). DG upwind flux. Spectral: filters for Gibbs. (9) Implementation: FD simplest (few lines). FEM complex (mesh generators, assemblers; FEniCS, deal.II libraries). Spectral moderate (FFT-based). (10) Parallel: FEM and DG naturally parallel (element-local). FD structured grid (good for vector/SIMD). Spectral FFT parallel (slab/pencil decomposition). Recommendations: heat/wave eq on rectangle -> FD or FEM. Elliptic on complex geometry -> FEM. Hyperbolic conservation with shocks -> DG or FV (WENO). Incompressible turbulence -> pseudo-spectral (Fourier). Compressible flow -> DG or WENO-FD. Structural mechanics -> FEM. Quantum/chemistry -> spectral. Weather/climate -> spectral element. General-purpose PDE: FEM (most flexible); use FD for prototyping; spectral for smooth problems. ✓',
  'ch07p5_method_comparison',
  'formula_recall',
  ['FD vs FEM vs spectral', 'choosing method', 'geometry smoothness', 'accuracy cost', 'adaptivity']
)

