add(
  'What is the multigrid method and why is it optimal for elliptic problems?',
  'Multigrid method. Idea: solve elliptic PDE (e.g., Poisson) on hierarchy of grids (fine -> coarse). Smooth error on fine grid (relaxation: Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel), then correct on coarse grid (where smooth error is well-resolved), recurse. Components: (1) Smoother: Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel, ILU. Reduces HIGH-frequency error (oscillatory) but not low-frequency (smooth). (2) Restriction: transfer residual from fine to coarse (full weighting, injection). (3) Prolongation: interpolate correction from coarse to fine (linear, bilinear). (4) Coarse-grid operator: discretize PDE on coarse grid (or Galerkin: A_coarse = R A_fine P). V-cycle: (1) Pre-smooth on fine (nu_1 iterations). (2) Compute residual r = b - A x. (3) Restrict r to coarse: r_c = R r. (4) Solve A_c e_c = r_c (recurse, or exact on coarsest). (5) Prolong e_c to fine: e = P e_c. (6) Correct x = x + e. (7) Post-smooth (nu_2 iterations). W-cycle: two coarse recursions per level (more expensive but more robust). Full Multigrid (FMG): start on coarsest, solve, prolong to finer, V-cycle; nest. Complexity: V-cycle O(N) work (one sweep). FMG O(N) total (optimal; converges in O(1) V-cycles to discretization accuracy). Compare: Jacobi O(N^2), SOR O(N^{1.5}), direct O(N^{1.5}-N^3). Convergence factor: rho < 0.1 typical per V-cycle (independent of N; "textbook multigrid"). Conditions: elliptic (smoother reduces high freq); regular grid/coarsening. For anisotropic problems (strong coefficient in one direction): semi-coarsening (coarsen only in strong direction) or alternating line smoothers. For complex geometry: algebraic multigrid (AMG): coarsen based on matrix entries, not geometry; works for unstructured. Geometric multigrid: uses mesh hierarchy. MG for nonlinear (FAS: Full Approximation Scheme): solve nonlinear on each level, no separate residual equation. MG for time-dependent: space-time MG, parabolic MG. MG as preconditioner: MGCG (CG accelerated by MG). Applications: Poisson (pressure Poisson in Navier-Stokes), linear elasticity, diffusion, Laplacian eigenvalue (Rayleigh quotient MG). Software: Hypre, ML (Trilinos), AMGCL, PyAMG. ✓',
  'ch07p5_multigrid',
  'formula_recall',
  ['multigrid', 'V-cycle W-cycle FMG', 'smoother restriction prolongation', 'O(N) optimal', 'AMG']
)

add(
  'What is the alternating direction implicit (ADI) method for parabolic PDEs?',
  'Alternating Direction Implicit (ADI) method. For 2D parabolic u_t = alpha (u_{xx} + u_{yy}). Direct implicit requires solving 2D system (large). ADI splits into two 1D implicit solves per step. Peaceman-Rachford (1955): U^* = U^n + (Delta t/2) alpha (delta_xx U^* + delta_yy U^n) (implicit in x, explicit in y). U^{n+1} = U^* + (Delta t/2) alpha (delta_xx U^* + delta_yy U^{n+1}) (implicit in y, explicit in x). Each sub-step is tridiagonal solve (fast). Second-order accurate O(Delta t^2 + Delta x^2 + Delta y^2). Unconditionally stable. Doublas-Rachford: variant for 3D (less accurate, O(Delta t)). Mitchell-Fairweather: higher-order. Fractional step (Yanenko): for general operator L = L_1 + L_2 + ..., split: (I - Delta t L_1) U^* = U^n; (I - Delta t L_2) U^{**} = U^*; ...; U^{n+1} = U^{(last)}. First-order accurate (unless corrections), unconditionally stable. Strang splitting (for time-dependent): symmetric splitting (half-step L_1, full L_2, half L_1) for second-order. ADI for 3D: u_t = alpha (u_{xx} + u_{yy} + u_{zz}). Three sub-steps, each tridiagonal in one direction. Cost: O(N) per step (vs O(N^{1.5}) for direct 3D implicit). Stability: unconditional. Accuracy: O(Delta t^2) for Peaceman-Rachford 2D; O(Delta t) for some 3D variants. Boundary conditions: intermediate values U^* need BC; use physical BC plus Delta t correction (Mitchell). Generalization: dimensional splitting for hyperbolic (dimensional ADI); LOD (locally one-dimensional). Applications: 2D/3D heat, diffusion, Black-Scholes multi-asset, reservoir simulation, groundwater. Software: standard in PDE libraries (FISHPACK, MUDPACK). Limitations: (1) Splits operator; may lose conservation. (2) BC handling subtle. (3) For systems, stability may degrade. (4) Coordinate-aligned (not for unstructured; use IMEX instead). IMEX (Implicit-Explicit): modern alternative; implicit for stiff (diffusion), explicit for non-stiff (advection); handles unstructured via FEM. ✓',
  'ch07p5_adi_method',
  'formula_recall',
  ['ADI', 'Peaceman-Rachford', 'alternating direction', 'tridiagonal solve', 'fractional step']
)

add(
  'What is the boundary element method (BEM) and how does it use Green\'s functions?',
  'Boundary Element Method (BEM). Idea: reformulate PDE as boundary integral equation (BIE) using Green\'s functions; discretize only the BOUNDARY (not the volume). Setup: Laplace or Poisson in domain D. Green\'s representation: u(x) = integral_{∂D} [G(x, y) partial u/partial n(y) - partial G/partial n(y) u(y)] dS_y (for x in D). Boundary limit (x -> ∂D): yields BIE relating u and partial u/partial n on ∂D. For Dirichlet (u given on ∂D): solve for partial u/partial n (hypersingular BIE). For Neumann: solve for u (Fredholm second-kind). Discretization: (1) Mesh ∂D into elements (panels). (2) Approximate u, partial u/partial n as piecewise polynomial on panels. (3) Collocate BIE at panel centers (or Galerkin). (4) Solve linear system. Matrix: dense (each panel interacts with all others via Green\'s function); N = number of boundary DOFs (smaller than FEM volume DOFs). Cost: O(N^3) direct solve, O(N^2) matrix-vector. Acceleration: fast multipole method (FMM) O(N log N) or O(N); panel clustering (Hackbusch); H-matrices. Advantages: (1) Dimensional reduction (3D -> 2D boundary; 2D -> 1D). (2) Infinite domain natural (no truncation; Green\'s function decays at infinity). (3) High accuracy for smooth boundaries (panel methods). (4) Stress concentration / gradients directly from boundary. Disadvantages: (1) Dense matrices (vs sparse FEM). (2) Needs fundamental solution (Laplace, Helmholtz, Stokes OK; variable coefficients hard). (3) Hypersingular integrals (need regularization). (4) Non-symmetric matrices (usually). Applications: external aerodynamics (panel methods, potential flow), acoustics (Helmholtz BEM), electromagnetics (MoM, EFIE/MFIE), fracture mechanics (crack tip), Stokes flow (boundary integral for suspensions), seismology. Variants: (a) Indirect BEM: use single/double layer potentials as ansatz. (b) Direct BEM: use Green\'s representation. (c) Dual reciprocity BEM (DRBEM): handle nonhomogeneous via particular solutions. Coupled FEM-BEM: BEM for exterior (infinite), FEM for interior (complex geometry). Nyström method: alternative discretization (quadrature on boundary). For Helmholtz (acoustics): avoid spurious resonances (Burton-Miller, combined field). ✓',
  'ch07p5_boundary_element',
  'formula_recall',
  ['BEM', 'boundary integral', 'Green\'s function', 'panel method', 'fast multipole']
)

add(
  'What is the fast multipole method (FMM) and how does it accelerate BEM?',
  'Fast Multipole Method (FMM), Greengard-Rokhlin 1987. Problem: N-body interactions (e.g., BEM matrix-vector product, particle simulations) cost O(N^2) naively. FMM reduces to O(N) (or O(N log N)). Idea: hierarchical grouping; far-field interactions computed via multipole expansions, near-field directly. Algorithm: (1) Build octree (3D) / quadtree (2D): recursively subdivide domain into boxes; each level has 8 (or 4) children. Stop when ~few points per leaf. (2) Multipole-to-local (M2L): for each box, the potential due to far-away sources is approximated by a multipole expansion (Legendre / spherical harmonic) centered at box center. Convert to local expansion around target box center. (3) Upward pass: compute multipole expansions at each level (leaf -> root), combining children expansions via translation (M2M). (4) Downward pass: compute local expansions at each level (root -> leaf), via L2L (local-to-local translation) and M2L. (5) Evaluate: at each target point, sum local expansion (far field) + direct interactions with neighbors (near field). Accuracy: controllable by expansion order p (number of multipoles); error ~ (a/d)^p where a = box size, d = distance; typical p = 10-20 for 1e-10 accuracy. Cost: O(N) for fixed p (each point interacts with O(1) boxes at each level; log N levels => O(N log N) or O(N) with proper FMM). Applications: BEM (matrix-vector product in iterative solver: GMRES + FMM), N-body simulations (stars, molecules), electrostatics (Poisson-Boltzmann), radar cross-section (MoM). Variants: (a) Black-box FMM (Martinsson): kernel-independent, uses Chebyshev interpolation. (b) Kernel-independent FMM (Ying-Biros-Zorin): uses equivalent source densities. (c) Periodic FMM (Ewald): for periodic BC (crystallography). (d) Adaptive FMM: variable tree depth (non-uniform distributions). (e) Directional FMM: for oscillatory kernels (Helmholtz high-frequency; Engquist-Ying). Modern: GPU FMM (fast), distributed FMM (petascale). Software: ExaFMM, ScalFMM, PVFMM. History: Greengard-Rokhlin 1987 (1e-6 speedup for 10^6 particles); now standard in computational physics. Without FMM: BEM limited to ~10^4 DOFs; with FMM: 10^7-10^8 DOFs. ✓',
  'ch07p5_fast_multipole',
  'formula_recall',
  ['FMM', 'fast multipole', 'Greengard-Rokhlin', 'multipole expansion', 'O(N) N-body']
)

add(
  'What are level set methods for interface tracking and free boundary problems?',
  'Level set method (Osher-Sethian 1988). Track moving interface Gamma(t) as zero level set of a higher-dimensional function phi(x, t): Gamma(t) = {x : phi(x, t) = 0}. phi > 0 one side, phi < 0 other side (signed distance function typically: phi = signed distance to Gamma). Motion: interface moves with velocity v(x, t) (normal component v_n). Level set equation: phi_t + v · grad phi = 0, or phi_t + v_n |grad phi| = 0 (v_n given; tangential doesn\'t affect interface). Solve as Hamilton-Jacobi PDE. Numerical: (1) High-order upwind (WENO, Godunov) for spatial discretization. (2) TVD-RK for time. (3) Reinitialization: |grad phi| = 1 (signed distance); solve d phi/d tau + sgn(phi_0)(|grad phi| - 1) = 0 (Sussman) to keep phi well-conditioned. (4) Extension of velocity: extend v_n off interface smoothly (PDE extension). Advantages: (1) Handles topology change (merging, splitting) automatically (no special logic). (2) Geometric quantities easy: normal n = grad phi/|grad phi|, curvature kappa = div(grad phi/|grad phi|). (3) 2D/3D unified. (4) High-order accurate. Disadvantages: (1) Higher-dim representation (2D interface -> 3D phi). (2) Mass loss (reinitialization can lose mass; fix: mass-conserving reinit). (3) Sharp interface smeared over few cells. Variants: (a) Fast marching method (FMM, Sethian): for stationary Eikonal |grad T| = 1/F (front propagation with speed F); O(N log N), one-shot (not time-evolving). (b) Narrow band level set: solve only near interface (O(N) per step). (c) Particle level set: combine with Lagrangian markers for sharpness. (d) Diffuse interface (phase field): alternative; Cahn-Hilliard for interface. Applications: (1) Two-phase flow (water-air, with surface tension). (2) Combustion (flame front). (3) Image processing (segmentation, Mumford-Shah). (4) Crystal growth, epitaxy. (5) Computer graphics (fluid animation, terrain). (6) Geometry (curve/surface evolution, mean curvature flow). (7) Optimization (shape optimization, level set method for topology). Coupled with Navier-Stokes: immersed interface, ghost fluid (Fedkiw) for sharp jump conditions. ✓',
  'ch07p5_level_set',
  'formula_recall',
  ['level set', 'Osher-Sethian', 'interface tracking', 'Hamilton-Jacobi', 'reinitialization']
)

